# Hibernate 6 Compatibility Guide

## Overview
Spring Boot 3.x uses Hibernate 6.x (specifically Hibernate ORM 6.1+), which includes significant changes from Hibernate 5.x. This guide covers the key compatibility considerations and migration steps.

## Major Changes in Hibernate 6

### 1. Jakarta Persistence API
- Migrated from `javax.persistence.*` to `jakarta.persistence.*`
- All JPA annotations and interfaces use Jakarta namespace

### 2. Query Language Updates
- HQL and JPQL improvements
- Better SQL standard compliance
- Enhanced function support

### 3. ID Generation
- New ID generation strategies
- `@GeneratedValue` behavior changes
- Better database-specific optimizations

### 4. Type System
- Redesigned type system
- Better handling of Java 8+ types
- Improved JSON support

### 5. Performance Improvements
- Better query performance
- Optimized batch processing
- Reduced memory footprint

## Migration Guide

### 1. JPA Annotations Migration

#### Before (Hibernate 5 / javax)
```java
import javax.persistence.*;

@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true)
    private String username;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "department_id")
    private Department department;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private List<Order> orders;
}
```

#### After (Hibernate 6 / jakarta)
```java
import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true)
    private String username;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "department_id")
    private Department department;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private List<Order> orders;
}
```

